---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ObfuscatedEmail from '../../../components/ObfuscatedEmail.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import coverSommerfest2026 from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260725_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Elisabeth_Blum_303.jpg';
import imgCrowd from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260724_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Johanna_Lea_Lassnig-09.jpg';
import imgSwkBleibt from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260724_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Elisabeth_Blum_229.jpg';
import imgNet from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260724_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Markus_Gesierich-15.jpg';
import imgClown from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260725_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Johanna_Lea_Lassnig-68.jpg';
import imgKritzelwald from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260724_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Markus_Gesierich-58.jpg';
import imgNight from '../../../assets/images/program/sommerfest-2026/260724_SWK_Sommerfest_c_Johanna_Lea_Lassnig-64.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Semmelweisklinik • Summer Festival 2026"
  description="Klinik unter Palmen 2026: a two-day summer festival at the Semmelweisklinik arts and culture centre in Vienna with music, variety, workshops and over 1,000 visitors."
  ogImage={coverSommerfest2026.src}
>
  <div class="page-wrapper">
    <div class="content container-narrow">
      <h1 class="hero-title">SUMMER FESTIVAL 2026</h1>
      <Image
        class="mt-50 full-width-image"
        src={coverSommerfest2026}
        alt="Visitors strolling between the festival stalls"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />

      <p class="mt-50">
        Already for the fourth time, the "Klinik unter Palmen" summer festival took place on the
        last weekend of July. For two days, the Semmelweisklinik became a lively place full of
        music, art, lovely encounters and children's laughter.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-20">
        With a lot of heart, love and sweat, the members of the Kulturverein Semmelweisklinik
        transform the former clinic grounds in Vienna's 18th district into a very special oasis for
        art, culture and community. And you can feel it at the festival: behind "Klinik unter
        Palmen" is a community that wants to create a place where everyone feels welcome.
      </p>
      <Image
        class="mt-30 full-width-image"
        src={imgCrowd}
        alt="Hand-painted signposts at the Summer Festival 2026"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />
      <h2 class="mt-50">Crazy Creatures</h2>
      <p class="mt-20">
        This year's motto, "Crazy Creatures", was impossible to miss on arrival. All over the
        grounds, visitors met funny, quirky and imaginative creatures, brought to life with great
        attention to detail by Ute Zaunbauer, Ebrahim Eskandari, Mia Mayer and other artists from
        the Semmelweisklinik, among others.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-20">
        A particular eye-catcher was the oversized fox head enthroned above the main stage, gazing
        down at the visitors. When the lights and fog set in during the evening, the stage turned
        into a downright magical backdrop.
      </p>
      <Image
        class="mt-30 full-width-image"
        src={imgSwkBleibt}
        alt="A cardboard sign reading SWK bleibt in the park"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />
      <h2 class="mt-50">Music, variety and plenty to discover</h2>
      <p class="mt-20">
        On the two outdoor stages, 19 acts provided a varied programme. Besides numerous concerts by
        Viennese artists, there was the variety show MIAO, a DJ workshop for FLINTA* people, dance
        performances by Dafne Moreno and a show by the rapper Koko Cappelle, whose grand performance
        moved the audience to cheering and tears.
      </p>
      <div class="portrait mt-30">
        <Image
          class="mt-30 full-width-image"
          src={imgNight}
          alt="Audience dancing in coloured light and fog"
          widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-20">
        But there was plenty to experience away from the stages as well. All over the grounds, small
        and large discoveries awaited: home-built mini-golf courses, a ball pit and a
        screen-printing station invited visitors to join in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-20">
        In the kids' corner, the youngest visitors could get creative in workshops and readings and
        watch a performance by Clown Wully. Under shady trees, a large canvas became a fairy-tale
        "Kritzelwald" (doodle forest), where small and large artists could immortalise themselves
        with their drawings. Anyone wanting to try something new could practise juggling with clubs
        and balls in the circus corner. In the nearby sculpture garden, visitors could discover
        works in clay and other materials by artists from the house, and take a little souvenir home
        at the end.
      </p>
      <Image
        class="mt-30 full-width-image"
        src={imgClown}
        alt="Clown Wully performing for children in the park"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />
      <Image
        class="mt-30 full-width-image"
        src={imgKritzelwald}
        alt="A child painting on the Kritzelwald canvas"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />
      <p class="mt-20">
        And anyone who needed a break from the hustle and bustle could simply laze in the shade in
        one of the many hammocks, or sit down on the big carpet of the Common Ground community,
        which keeps growing through continuous weaving, and chat with others.
      </p>
      <Image
        class="mt-30 full-width-image"
        src={imgNet}
        alt="A visitor resting in a net of cords strung between trees"
        widths={[600, 1000, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px"
      />
      <p class="mt-20">
        In the cool laundry room, the Radical Relaxation Resort was a very special retreat. Besides
        an exhibition by various artists, visitors could relax with a foot bath. There were also
        fantastic, extra-galactic laser projections that made the walls glow.
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-50">A festival that connects</h2>
      <p class="mt-20">
        More than 1,000 visitors came over the two days and celebrated the existence of the Kunst-
        und Kulturverein Semmelweisklinik together with us. It is a place that we want to keep
        filling with life, art, culture and lovely encounters in the future.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-20">
        A festival like this is only possible thanks to the many people who contributed their time,
        their ideas and their energy: 28 artists helped shape the grounds and the programme, and 62
        Helping Hands pulled the festival off, from planning through set-up and supervision to
        cleaning up together. In the end, what remains are not only memories of two beautiful summer
        days, but above all the feeling of having created something special together.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-20">
        Thank you to everyone who was there, took part, helped, marvelled, danced, laughed and
        filled this place with life. We are already looking forward to next time!
      </p>

      <p class="mt-30 text-12">Photos: Elisabeth Blum, Johanna Lea Lassnig, Markus Gesierich</p>

      <p class="mt-40">
        Press and general inquiries:<br />
        <ObfuscatedEmail address="info@semmelweisklinik.at" />
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .portrait {
    max-width: 560px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .portrait :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
